[[tree-sitter-parsing]]
Parsing source with tree-sitter

Tree-sitter is an incremental parsing library with grammars for a wide range of
programming languages. It produces a concrete syntax tree and, crucially, tolerates
incomplete or malformed input — a property that matters because real source files are
edited mid-thought and rarely parse cleanly at every keystroke. When a file changes,
the parser reuses the unchanged parts of the previous tree instead of starting over.

A graph builder walks the syntax tree to extract the symbols it cares about:
definitions, signatures, and the spans of source they occupy. Because the same
extraction front-end can be pointed at many grammars, one pipeline covers dozens of
languages without a separate analyzer per language. Documentation formats are parsed
the same way, yielding headings, sections, and cross-references as nodes.
